
  [1mC. HTML-native constraints[0m
  Put the rules on the markup — [38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminlength="8"[39m, [38;5;153mtype="email"[39m, [38;5;153mpattern="..."[39m — and use the browser's Constraint
  Validation API ([38;5;153minput.validity[39m, [38;5;153mcheckValidity()[39m). JS supplies only friendly messages and renders them.
  [3mBuys:[0m the least new code by a wide margin; rules live next to the fields; works before JS loads.
  [3mCosts:[0m you're limited to what HTML expresses (no range-across-fields, awkward custom rules), the default messages are
  browser-specific and need overriding one by one, and — the real problem for this task — there's almost nothing left to
  unit-test, which conflicts with the [38;5;153mnode:test[39m choice you just made.

  [1mWhy A:[0m it's the only one of the three where "add a rule nobody anticipated" costs a single function, and it keeps the
  core a pure, DOM-free function that [38;5;153mnode:test[39m can exercise directly. B's serializability buys nothing here — there's
  no server and no config source. C is genuinely the least code, but it trades away the testable core and caps what the
  rule set can ever express.

  A worthwhile piece of C is compatible with A and I'd fold it in: the binder reads field values by [38;5;153mname[39m falling back to
  [38;5;153mid[39m, so the markup stays the source of truth for [3mwhich[0m fields exist while the schema owns [3mwhat's valid[0m.

